            "review_title": "Review at Narak Thai Kitchen",
            "review_text": "thummers\nSan Jose, California9 contributions\n0\nThai flavors remixed for the Vietnamese palate. They went viral, and deservingly so.\nJul 2025 • Family\nTL;DR - Thai flavors remixed for the Vietnamese palate. They went viral, and deservingly so.\n\nIf Narak sounds familiar, you’re not imagining things. This restaurant blew up on Vietnamese Facebook and TikTok. The demand was so overwhelming, they had to relocate to a new space twice the size (no small feat in Saigon). That buzz is very well earned.\n\nThe new space balances intimacy and energy. Tables are spaced far enough for conversation, and close enough for ambiance. The decor leans minimalist, with food-focused lighting, illustrated dishes on the wall, flowers in vases, and plants in cement bricks. Nothing flashy, because they know what you’re here for.\n\nLet’s get on to the most important part - the food. The chefs clearly know their Thai cooking foundations and made thoughtful tradeoffs for a Vietnamese crowd. They traded in the bold and punchy for the clean and restrained. The curries are lighter, the broths cleaner, sour notes gentler, and the sweetness subdued. \nThe flavors are all still there, tapping you gently, and hugging you on the way out.\n\nThe standout dishes were the soft boiled tamarind eggs (Trung Long Dao Sot Me) with a crisped exterior I’ve never had elsewhere, the Soft Shell Crab (Cua Lot Sot Me) fried light and draped in sauce without feeling heavy, and the grilled pork neck (Co Heo Nuong) which was smoky, tender, and balanced. I walked out feeling full, but not heavy.\n\nNarak isn’t trying to be “authentic” for the sake of it - it’s Thai food, reimagined for Saigonese, and it’s a bullseye.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nAtmosphere\nWritten August 26, 2025",
            "rating": 5.0,
            "review_date": "2026-10-14",
            "travel_date": "Recent",
            "feature_depth_status": "Schema-Full-Features-Validated"
